.\" beryllium-sandbox(7): what Beryllium confines, by what means, and how
.\" to check it.
.\" Source: man/beryllium-sandbox.7 in the Beryllium repository.
.\" The port replaces @@VERSION@@ and the /usr/local prefix at install.
.\" Every statement here must agree with the feature matrix
.\" (plan/13-FEATURE-MATRIX.md); this page claims nothing a test does not
.\" back.
.\" One sentence per line; check with freebsd/tools/check-man.
.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt BERYLLIUM-SANDBOX 7
.Os Beryllium @@VERSION@@
.Sh NAME
.Nm beryllium-sandbox
.Nd process confinement in the Beryllium web browser
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr beryllium 1
runs as several processes.
The browser process owns the windows, the profile and the network
identity of the user.
A renderer runs the content of one site.
A GPU process draws, and utility processes handle the network, storage
and audio.
A sandbox confines the processes that handle untrusted data, so that a
fault in one of them does not give a web page the authority of the user's
account.
.Pp
This page states what Beryllium enforces, by what means, and how to check
it from outside the browser.
It describes the release it is installed with.
.Ss Status
By default no process is confined.
A compromised web page is not contained, as with the Chromium port for
.Fx .
.Bl -column "Network, storage, audio" "Status" -offset indent
.It Sy Process Ta Sy Status
.It Browser Ta not confined
.It Renderers Ta not confined by default; lockdown below
.It GPU Ta not confined
.It Network, storage, audio Ta not confined
.El
.Pp
The page
.Li beryllium://gpu
reports
.Dq Sandboxed: false
for the GPU process, which is the kernel's answer and not a claim.
.Ss Measures in every process
Three measures apply to every child process, whether or not the lockdown
is on.
They limit what a compromised process can reach.
They do not confine it.
.Bl -bullet
.It
The descriptors the browser has open are closed between
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr execve 2 ,
so a child starts with the descriptors meant for it and no others.
.It
Shared memory is anonymous, made with
.Xr memfd_create 2 :
it has no name in any file system, its size is sealed, and a region
shared read-only is read-only by its Capsicum rights.
.It
A renderer is given no swap file, so page content parked by a background
tab stays in memory.
.El
.Ss The renderer lockdown
With the feature
.Cm BerylliumCapsicumRenderer
enabled, each renderer does the following before it handles page content:
.Bl -enum
.It
It reads what capability mode would refuse later and keeps the answers:
time zone data, message catalogues, the amount of memory.
.It
It judges every descriptor it holds by the admission rule below and cuts
each one it keeps to the rights of its kind.
.It
It limits its standard streams.
.It
It calls
.Xr cap_enter 2
and checks the result with
.Xr cap_getmode 2 .
.El
.Pp
A process in capability mode cannot open a file by path, cannot create a
network connection and cannot signal another process; see
.Xr capsicum 4 .
What it can still do is decided by the descriptors it holds and their
rights, which is why the policy below matters as much as the mode.
.Pp
If any step fails, the renderer ends.
A renderer asked to confine itself never runs unconfined.
.Ss Descriptor policy
At the lockdown point a descriptor is admitted by what it is and where it
comes from:
.Bl -bullet
.It
A read-only file is admitted only under
.Pa /usr/local/share/beryllium/ ,
the browser's own resources.
.It
A socket is admitted only as an end of an unnamed
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket pair, stream or sequenced packet, bound to no path and connected
to no path.
.It
A directory is closed.
.It
Anything else, such as a writable file, a device or a terminal, ends the
renderer.
.El
.Pp
Each kind keeps these rights and no others; see
.Xr rights 4 :
.Bl -tag -width "shared memory"
.It read-only file
.Dv CAP_PREAD ,
.Dv CAP_MMAP_R ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL .
.It socket
.Dv CAP_RECV ,
.Dv CAP_SEND ,
.Dv CAP_EVENT ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
.Dv CAP_SHUTDOWN ,
.Dv CAP_GETSOCKOPT ,
.Dv CAP_SETSOCKOPT ,
and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL
and
.Dv F_SETFL .
Never the rights to connect, bind, listen or accept.
.It kqueue
.Dv CAP_KQUEUE ,
.Dv CAP_EVENT
and
.Dv CAP_FSTAT .
.It eventfd, pipe
.Dv CAP_READ ,
.Dv CAP_WRITE ,
.Dv CAP_EVENT ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL
and
.Dv F_SETFL ,
and
.Dv CAP_IOCTL
for
.Dv FIONREAD
alone.
.It shared memory
.Dv CAP_READ ,
.Dv CAP_WRITE ,
.Dv CAP_SEEK ,
.Dv CAP_MMAP_RW ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL ,
less whatever its creator withheld.
.It writable file
Only one the browser hands over on purpose after the lockdown, such as a
file a site was granted through the File System Access interface:
.Dv CAP_PREAD ,
.Dv CAP_PWRITE ,
.Dv CAP_FTRUNCATE ,
.Dv CAP_FSYNC ,
.Dv CAP_MMAP_RW ,
.Dv CAP_FSTAT ,
and
.Dv CAP_FCNTL
for
.Dv F_GETFL .
.El
.Pp
Rights are only ever reduced, with
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_fcntls_limit 2
and
.Xr cap_ioctls_limit 2 .
.Pp
Capsicum gives a new descriptor every right, so the policy is applied
wherever a descriptor appears after the lockdown as well:
.Bl -bullet
.It
What a confined renderer creates is cut at creation: the kqueue and
eventfd of each message loop, shared memory, and the socket pairs Mojo
makes.
.It
What it receives over a Mojo channel is cut on arrival.
A received descriptor the policy has no kind for keeps no right at all.
.It
The browser cuts every descriptor it sends a confined renderer before it
sends it, so the limits do not depend on the renderer's cooperation.
.El
.Ss What is not covered
The lockdown is experimental, off by default, and not yet a complete
sandbox.
.Bl -bullet
.It
The browser, GPU, network, storage and audio processes are not confined.
.It
What the GPU process sends a renderer directly is limited by the
receiving renderer only.
.It
The browser does not yet ask the kernel whether a renderer is confined
before giving it a page; the checks in
.Sx EXAMPLES
are made from outside.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check that every renderer is in capability mode, shown by a
.Sq C
in the flags column:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procstat -s $(pgrep -f -- --type=renderer)
.Ed
.Pp
List the descriptors of one renderer with their Capsicum rights:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
procstat -fC 12345
.Ed
.Pp
Both need
.Va security.bsd.unprivileged_proc_debug
set to 1.
.Pp
From a checkout of the repository, run the sandbox tests with the lockdown
on.
They then act as a gate: they fail if any renderer is not in capability
mode by the kernel's answer, if its descriptors cannot be read, or if any
descriptor is outside the policy.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
freebsd/tests/browser/run sandbox \e
    --browser-arg=--enable-features=BerylliumCapsicumRenderer
.Ed
.Pp
Record what capability mode refuses in each process, with
.Xr ktrace 1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
freebsd/tests/browser/run capsicum --diagnose \e
    --browser-arg=--enable-features=BerylliumCapsicumRenderer
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The lockdown writes to the browser's log, which
.Fl -enable-logging Ns = Ns Cm stderr
sends to standard error.
.Bl -diag
.It "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N capability_mode=1 closed_directories=N limited=N threads=N thread_names=..."
A renderer locked down.
The line counts the directories it closed and the descriptors it limited,
and gives the number and the names of the threads the process had at that
point.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N could not enter capability mode: ..."
The lockdown failed and the renderer ended.
The text after the colon names the descriptor that broke the policy, or
the call that failed.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: received ... (fd N), which a confined process may not hold: every right removed"
A confined renderer received a descriptor the policy has no kind for.
The descriptor stays open with no rights.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: socket pair made at ... on thread '...' (fd N, fd N): limited"
A confined renderer made a socket pair; both ends were cut to a socket's
rights.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: cut ... (fd N) bound for confined renderer pid N"
The browser cut a descriptor before sending it.
Logged only with
.Fl -vmodule Ns = Ns Cm capsicum_rights=1 .
.It "beryllium-sandbox: ... bound for confined renderer pid N, which it may not hold: every right removed before sending"
The browser was about to send a confined renderer a descriptor the policy
has no kind for.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr beryllium 1 ,
.Xr kdump 1 ,
.Xr ktrace 1 ,
.Xr procstat 1 ,
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr cap_fcntls_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_ioctls_limit 2 ,
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2 ,
.Xr memfd_create 2 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr rights 4
.Rs
.%A Robert N. M. Watson
.%A Jonathan Anderson
.%A Ben Laurie
.%A Kris Kennaway
.%T "Capsicum: practical capabilities for UNIX"
.%B "Proceedings of the 19th USENIX Security Symposium"
.%D August 2010
.Re
.Pp
The plan documents in the repository, in particular
.Pa plan/06-SECURITY-MODEL.md
and
.Pa plan/14-RENDERER-SANDBOX.md ,
give the reasoning and the results of each build.
.Sh HISTORY
The renderer lockdown first appeared in Beryllium 0.20.3.3.
The closing of inherited descriptors followed in 0.20.3.4, anonymous
shared memory in 0.20.3.5, the descriptor policy in 0.20.3.8, admission by
place and shape in 0.20.3.10, limits at creation in 0.20.3.12 and the
browser's limits before sending in 0.20.3.13.
.Sh CAVEATS
A process in capability mode cannot write a core dump.
To see which call a confined renderer was refused, enable
.Cm BerylliumCapsicumTrap
as well, or record the refusals with the test suite as shown above.
.Pp
The status table and
.Sx What is not covered
are the honest summary: do not rely on this release to contain a
compromised web page.
